<graph edgedefault="undirected"><node id="Weighted Shortest Job First (WSJF)">
  <data key="d0">Weighted Shortest Job First (WSJF)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">WSJF is a prioritization model used by Agile teams and organizations to sequence jobs based on the cost of delay and job duration, aiming to deliver maximum economic benefit in the shortest time.&lt;SEP&gt;WSJF is a prioritization model used in Agile to sequence jobs based on the cost of delay and job duration, maximizing economic benefit.&lt;SEP&gt;Weighted Shortest Job First (WSJF) is a prioritization model widely used by Agile teams and organizations to sequence jobs in a way that maximizes economic benefit. The model operates by evaluating two key factors: the cost of delay (CoD) and the job duration. By prioritizing jobs with the highest economic value relative to their time investment, WSJF helps teams deliver maximum economic benefit in the shortest possible time. This approach is particularly effective in Agile environments, where efficient decision-making and value delivery are critical.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4df9cb7b8cf1803448d29a16d8b2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d07decf500a728985eee0f94204ebaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cbea2cfe8c2736273a2f8aa2beb621f</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851987</data>
</node>
<node id="Cost of Delay (CoD)">
  <data key="d0">Cost of Delay (CoD)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Cost of Delay (CoD) is a critical component of Weighted Shortest Job First (WSJF), a prioritization framework used in decision-making processes. It quantifies the economic impact of delaying a job by evaluating three key factors: User/Business Value, Time Criticality, and Risk Reduction or Opportunity Enablement. These factors are combined to calculate the Cost of Delay, providing a comprehensive measure of the urgency and importance of completing a task or project. By incorporating these elements, Cost of Delay helps organizations prioritize work effectively to maximize value and minimize potential losses associated with delays.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4df9cb7b8cf1803448d29a16d8b2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d07decf500a728985eee0f94204ebaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cbea2cfe8c2736273a2f8aa2beb621f</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851990</data>
</node>
<node id="User/Business Value">
  <data key="d0">User/Business Value</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">User/Business Value measures how much value a job delivers to the customer or business, often scored on a relative scale.&lt;SEP&gt;User/Business Value measures the value a job delivers to the customer or business, often scored on a relative scale.&lt;SEP&gt;User/Business Value measures the value a job delivers to customers or businesses, often scored on a relative scale.&lt;SEP&gt;User/Business Value measures the value a job delivers to customers or businesses, often scored on a relative scale.&lt;SEP&gt;User/Business Value measures the value a job delivers to customers or businesses, often scored on a relative scale.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4df9cb7b8cf1803448d29a16d8b2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d07decf500a728985eee0f94204ebaf</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851990</data>
</node>
<node id="Time Criticality">
  <data key="d0">Time Criticality</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Time Criticality assesses the urgency of delivering a job, considering factors like regulatory deadlines or market windows.&lt;SEP&gt;Time Criticality is a component of WSJF that measures the urgency of completing a job based on its impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Time Criticality is a concept that evaluates the urgency and importance of delivering a job promptly, taking into account factors such as regulatory deadlines or market windows. It is a key component of Weighted Shortest Job First (WSJF), a prioritization framework, and measures the urgency of completing a job based on its potential impact.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4df9cb7b8cf1803448d29a16d8b2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d07decf500a728985eee0f94204ebaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cbea2cfe8c2736273a2f8aa2beb621f</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851990</data>
</node>
<node id="Risk Reduction / Opportunity Enablement">
  <data key="d0">Risk Reduction / Opportunity Enablement</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Risk Reduction / Opportunity Enablement evaluates whether a job reduces future risks or enables new business opportunities.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk Reduction / Opportunity Enablement evaluates whether a job reduces future risks or enables new business opportunities.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk Reduction / Opportunity Enablement evaluates whether a job reduces future risks or enables new business opportunities.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk Reduction/Opportunity Enablement evaluates whether a job reduces future risks or enables new business opportunities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4df9cb7b8cf1803448d29a16d8b2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d07decf500a728985eee0f94204ebaf</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851990</data>
</node>
<node id="Job Size (Duration)">
  <data key="d0">Job Size (Duration)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Job Size estimates the effort required to complete a job, often measured in story points or team weeks.&lt;SEP&gt;Job Size represents the effort required to complete a job, estimated in story points or team weeks, with smaller jobs generally receiving higher WSJF scores.&lt;SEP&gt;Job Size represents the effort required to complete a job, estimated in story points or team weeks, with smaller jobs generally receiving higher WSJF scores.&lt;SEP&gt;Job Size represents the effort required to complete a job, estimated in story points or team weeks, with smaller jobs generally receiving higher WSJF scores.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4df9cb7b8cf1803448d29a16d8b2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d07decf500a728985eee0f94204ebaf</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851990</data>
</node>
<node id="Normalized Story Points">
  <data key="d0">Normalized Story Points</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Normalized Story Points are a relative estimation technique used by Agile teams to size user stories based on effort, complexity, and risk, standardized across teams.&lt;SEP&gt;Normalized Story Points are a standardized estimation technique used by Agile teams to size user stories based on effort, complexity, and risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Normalized Story Points are a standardized estimation technique used by Agile teams to size user stories based on effort, complexity, and risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Normalized Story Points are a standardized estimation technique used by Agile teams to size user stories based on effort, complexity, and risk.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4df9cb7b8cf1803448d29a16d8b2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d07decf500a728985eee0f94204ebaf</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851990</data>
</node>
<node id="Relative Story Points">
  <data key="d0">Relative Story Points</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Relative Story Points are a method of estimating effort, complexity, and risk by comparing user stories to others, using the Fibonacci sequence for scaling.&lt;SEP&gt;Relative Story Points are a method of estimating user stories by comparing their effort, complexity, and risk to other stories, using the Fibonacci sequence.&lt;SEP&gt;Relative Story Points are a method of estimating user stories by comparing their effort, complexity, and risk to other stories, using the Fibonacci sequence.&lt;SEP&gt;Relative Story Points are a method of estimating user stories by comparing their effort, complexity, and risk to other stories, using the Fibonacci sequence.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4df9cb7b8cf1803448d29a16d8b2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d07decf500a728985eee0f94204ebaf</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851990</data>
</node>
<node id="Fibonacci Sequence">
  <data key="d0">Fibonacci Sequence</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Fibonacci Sequence (1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21) is used in Agile estimation to reflect increasing uncertainty and encourage breaking down large work items.&lt;SEP&gt;The Fibonacci Sequence (1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21) is used in Agile estimation to reflect increasing uncertainty and encourage breaking down large work items.&lt;SEP&gt;The Fibonacci Sequence (1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21) is used in Agile estimation to reflect increasing uncertainty and encourage breaking down large work items.&lt;SEP&gt;The Fibonacci Sequence (1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21) is used in Agile estimation to reflect increasing uncertainty and encourage breaking down large work items.&lt;SEP&gt;The Fibonacci Sequence (1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21) is used in Agile estimation to reflect non-linear increases in size and uncertainty.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4df9cb7b8cf1803448d29a16d8b2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d07decf500a728985eee0f94204ebaf</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851990</data>
</node>
<node id="Design Thinking">
  <data key="d0">Design Thinking</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Design Thinking is a human-centered, iterative problem-solving approach that emphasizes empathy, creativity, and experimentation to create innovative solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Design Thinking is a human-centered, iterative problem-solving approach that emphasizes empathy, creativity, and experimentation to create innovative solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Design Thinking is a human-centered, iterative problem-solving approach that emphasizes empathy, creativity, and experimentation. It is designed to address complex challenges and create innovative solutions in a user-focused way. By integrating user needs, technological possibilities, and business requirements, Design Thinking helps redefine problems and develop solutions that are both practical and impactful. This approach prioritizes understanding user needs and encourages continuous experimentation to foster creativity and innovation.&lt;SEP&gt;Design Thinking is a human-centered, iterative problem-solving approach used to understand user needs, redefine problems, and create innovative solutions. It emphasizes empathy, creativity, and experimentation to solve complex challenges in a user-focused way.&lt;SEP&gt;Design Thinking is a human-centered, iterative problem-solving approach used to understand user needs, redefine problems, and create innovative solutions. It emphasizes empathy, creativity, and experimentation to solve complex challenges in a user-focused way.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fae7cb2d4758c5e268c9d6aa4f2febeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df9cb7b8cf1803448d29a16d8b2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9225b4d28906f88497be40499ee4eb1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d07decf500a728985eee0f94204ebaf</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851990</data>
</node>
