  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Tool overload refers to the excessive number of tools used by Sarah Martinez's team, which complicates workflows.&lt;SEP&gt;Tool overload refers to the excessive number of tools used by Sarah Martinez's team, which complicates workflows.&lt;SEP&gt;Tool overload refers to the excessive number of tools used by Sarah Martinez's team, which complicates workflows.&lt;SEP&gt;Tool overload refers to the frustration caused by using too many tools, leading to inefficiency and confusion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3100e8f6ca4cefc55be646b9ce12301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0158cac62eeeb668ef8f780b56285f7</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851990</data>
</node>
<node id="Difficult Onboarding">
  <data key="d0">Difficult Onboarding</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Difficult onboarding refers to challenges faced by new team members in adapting to tools and workflows.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3100e8f6ca4cefc55be646b9ce12301</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752657194</data>
</node>
<node id="Poor Integration">
  <data key="d0">Poor Integration</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Poor integration refers to the lack of seamless connectivity between tools and reporting systems, causing inefficiencies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3100e8f6ca4cefc55be646b9ce12301</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752657194</data>
</node>
<node id="Team Collaboration">
  <data key="d0">Team Collaboration</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Team collaboration involves working together effectively to achieve shared goals, often facilitated by tools like Slack.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3100e8f6ca4cefc55be646b9ce12301</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752657194</data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-Team Visibility">
  <data key="d0">Cross-Team Visibility</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Cross-team visibility refers to the ability to share and access information across different teams, a key goal for Sarah Martinez.&lt;SEP&gt;Cross-team visibility refers to the ability to share and access information across different teams, a key goal for Sarah Martinez.&lt;SEP&gt;Cross-team visibility refers to the ability to share and access information across different teams, a key goal for Sarah Martinez.&lt;SEP&gt;Cross-team visibility refers to the ability to track and understand activities across different teams to improve coordination.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3100e8f6ca4cefc55be646b9ce12301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0158cac62eeeb668ef8f780b56285f7</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851990</data>
</node>
<node id="Feature Prioritization">
  <data key="d0">Feature Prioritization</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Feature prioritization involves deciding which product features to focus on based on user needs and persona insights.&lt;SEP&gt;Feature prioritization is the process of deciding which product features to focus on based on user needs and business goals.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of deciding which features to focus on in product development based on user needs and goals.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of deciding which features to focus on in product development based on user needs and goals.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of deciding which features to focus on in product development based on user needs and goals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3100e8f6ca4cefc55be646b9ce12301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0158cac62eeeb668ef8f780b56285f7</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851990</data>
</node>
<node id="Marketing Messaging">
  <data key="d0">Marketing Messaging</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Marketing messaging involves crafting communication strategies to align with user personas and promote products effectively.&lt;SEP&gt;Marketing messaging refers to the communication strategies used to align with user personas and effectively convey the value of a product.&lt;SEP&gt;Strategies used to communicate the value of a product to users, often informed by user personas.&lt;SEP&gt;Strategies used to communicate the value of a product to users, often informed by user personas.&lt;SEP&gt;Strategies used to communicate the value of a product to users, often informed by user personas.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3100e8f6ca4cefc55be646b9ce12301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0158cac62eeeb668ef8f780b56285f7</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851990</data>
</node>
<node id="Risk Reduction/Opportunity Enablement">
  <data key="d0">Risk Reduction/Opportunity Enablement</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">This component evaluates whether a job reduces future risks or enables new business opportunities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4df9cb7b8cf1803448d29a16d8b2927f</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752658685</data>
</node>
<node id="Relative Story Point Estimation">
  <data key="d0">Relative Story Point Estimation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Relative Story Point Estimation is a method used by Agile teams to estimate effort, complexity, and risk by comparing user stories rather than assigning absolute time values.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4df9cb7b8cf1803448d29a16d8b2927f</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752658685</data>
</node>
<node id="Cost Of Delay (CoD)">
  <data key="d0">Cost Of Delay (CoD)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Cost Of Delay is a key component of WSJF, calculated as the sum of User/Business Value, Time Criticality, and Risk Reduction or Opportunity Enablement.&lt;SEP&gt;Cost Of Delay is a key component of WSJF, calculated as the sum of User/Business Value, Time Criticality, and Risk Reduction or Opportunity Enablement.&lt;SEP&gt;Cost Of Delay is a key component of WSJF, calculated as the sum of User/Business Value, Time Criticality, and Risk Reduction or Opportunity Enablement.&lt;SEP&gt;Cost Of Delay is a key component of WSJF, calculated as the sum of User/Business Value, Time Criticality, and Risk Reduction/Opportunity Enablement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4df9cb7b8cf1803448d29a16d8b2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d07decf500a728985eee0f94204ebaf</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851990</data>
</node>
<node id="Regulatory Deadlines">
  <data key="d0">Regulatory Deadlines</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Regulatory Deadlines are time-sensitive requirements imposed by governing bodies that influence the prioritization of jobs.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulatory Deadlines are time-sensitive requirements imposed by governing bodies that influence the prioritization of jobs.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulatory Deadlines are time-sensitive requirements imposed by governing bodies that influence the prioritization of jobs.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulatory Deadlines are time-sensitive requirements that influence the prioritization of tasks based on compliance needs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4df9cb7b8cf1803448d29a16d8b2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d07decf500a728985eee0f94204ebaf</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851990</data>
</node>
<node id="Market Windows">
  <data key="d0">Market Windows</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Market Windows are time-sensitive opportunities for delivering products or services to maximize business impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Market Windows are time-sensitive opportunities in the market that influence the urgency of delivering jobs.&lt;SEP&gt;Market Windows are time-sensitive opportunities in the market that influence the urgency of delivering jobs.&lt;SEP&gt;Market Windows are time-sensitive opportunities in the market that influence the urgency of delivering jobs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4df9cb7b8cf1803448d29a16d8b2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d07decf500a728985eee0f94204ebaf</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851990</data>
</node>
<node id="Risk Complexity Levels">
  <data key="d0">Risk Complexity Levels</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Risk Complexity Levels describe the varying degrees of complexity in tasks or projects, ranging from simple logic to epic-level challenges requiring decomposition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9225b4d28906f88497be40499ee4eb1f</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752658685</data>
</node>
<node id="Slightly More Complex">
  <data key="d0">Slightly More Complex</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Slightly More Complex tasks involve simple logic or UI with few edge cases.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9225b4d28906f88497be40499ee4eb1f</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752658685</data>
</node>
<node id="Moderate Complexity">
  <data key="d0">Moderate Complexity</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Moderate Complexity tasks include some unknowns and basic integration efforts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9225b4d28906f88497be40499ee4eb1f</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752658685</data>
</node>
<node id="High Complexity">
  <data key="d0">High Complexity</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">High Complexity tasks require cross-functional coordination, integration, or testing efforts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9225b4d28906f88497be40499ee4eb1f</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752658685</data>
</node>
<node id="Very Complex">
  <data key="d0">Very Complex</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Very Complex tasks demand significant coordination and carry a risk of rework.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9225b4d28906f88497be40499ee4eb1f</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752658685</data>
</node>
<node id="Extremely Complex">
  <data key="d0">Extremely Complex</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Extremely Complex tasks are too large for a sprint and require splitting due to significant unknowns.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9225b4d28906f88497be40499ee4eb1f</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752658685</data>
</node>
<node id="Epic-Level Complexity">
  <data key="d0">Epic-Level Complexity</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Epic-Level Complexity tasks are unclear and almost certainly need decomposition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9225b4d28906f88497be40499ee4eb1f</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752658685</data>
</node>
<node id="Goals And Objectives">
  <data key="d0">Goals And Objectives</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Goals and objectives refer to the key achievements or outcomes that a persona or user aims to accomplish, such as keeping team projects on schedule and improving cross-team visibility.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3100e8f6ca4cefc55be646b9ce12301</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752658685</data>
</node>
<node id="Pain Points">
  <data key="d0">Pain Points</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Pain points are the frustrations, blockers, or challenges faced by a persona, such as tool overload, difficult onboarding, and poor integration with reporting systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3100e8f6ca4cefc55be646b9ce12301</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752658685</data>
</node>
<node id="Behaviors">
  <data key="d0">Behaviors</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Behaviors describe how a persona interacts with a product or service, including their attitudes, decision-making influences, and tools they commonly use.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3100e8f6ca4cefc55be646b9ce12301</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752658685</data>
</node>
<node id="Tools And Devices">
  <data key="d0">Tools And Devices</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Tools and devices refer to the platforms, software, and hardware commonly used by a persona, such as laptops, smartphones, Slack, Jira, Excel, and Salesforce.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3100e8f6ca4cefc55be646b9ce12301</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752658685</data>
</node>
<node id="Quotes Or Mindset">
  <data key="d0">Quotes Or Mindset</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Quotes or mindset capture key expressions or attitudes that reflect a persona's thinking, such as Sarah Martinez's quote: 'I need tools that help my team get work done, not add more work.'</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3100e8f6ca4cefc55be646b9ce12301</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752658685</data>
</node>
<node id="Persona Template">
  <data key="d0">Persona Template</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The persona template is a structured framework used to define and document user personas, including their goals, pain points, behaviors, and technology use.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3100e8f6ca4cefc55be646b9ce12301</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752658685</data>
</node>
<node id="Best Practices">
  <data key="d0">Best Practices</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Best practices refer to recommended approaches for creating effective personas, such as basing them on research, keeping them focused, and updating them periodically.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3100e8f6ca4cefc55be646b9ce12301</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752658685</data>
</node>
<node id="Empathy">
  <data key="d0">Empathy</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Empathy is the ability to understand and share the feelings of users, which is a key reason why personas are created.&lt;SEP&gt;Empathy refers to the ability to understand and share the feelings of users, a key goal of creating user personas.&lt;SEP&gt;Empathy refers to the ability to understand and share the feelings of users, a key goal of creating user personas.&lt;SEP&gt;Empathy refers to the ability to understand and share the feelings of users, a key goal of creating user personas.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3100e8f6ca4cefc55be646b9ce12301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0158cac62eeeb668ef8f780b56285f7</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851990</data>
</node>
<node id="User Onboarding">
  <data key="d0">User Onboarding</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The process of helping new users understand and effectively use a product, often improved by insights from user personas.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of helping new users understand and effectively use a product, often improved by insights from user personas.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of helping new users understand and effectively use a product, often improved by insights from user personas.&lt;SEP&gt;User onboarding is the process of helping new users understand and adopt a product, guided by persona insights.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3100e8f6ca4cefc55be646b9ce12301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0158cac62eeeb668ef8f780b56285f7</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851990</data>
</node>
<node id="Team Alignment">
  <data key="d0">Team Alignment</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Team alignment refers to the process of ensuring all team members share a common understanding of the user, often achieved through personas.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of ensuring all team members share a common understanding of user needs and goals, often facilitated by personas.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of ensuring all team members share a common understanding of user needs and goals, often facilitated by personas.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of ensuring all team members share a common understanding of user needs and goals, often facilitated by personas.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3100e8f6ca4cefc55be646b9ce12301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0158cac62eeeb668ef8f780b56285f7</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851990</data>
</node>
<node id="Qualitative And Quantitative Research">
  <data key="d0">Qualitative And Quantitative Research</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Qualitative and quantitative research are methods used to gather data about users, combining non-numerical insights with numerical patterns.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3100e8f6ca4cefc55be646b9ce12301</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752658685</data>
</node>
<node id="User Story">
  <data key="d0">User Story</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A User Story is a small, well-defined unit of work in Agile development, written in the format 'As a [role], I want [activity] so that [business value].' It follows INVEST criteria and includes acceptance criteria, business value, dependencies, and testability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851935</data>
</node>
<node id="Feature">
  <data key="d0">Feature</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Feature, in the context of Agile development, represents a larger unit of work designed to deliver business or customer value. It is traceable to strategic themes, ensuring alignment with broader organizational goals, and is sized to fit within a Program Increment, making it manageable within a specific time frame. Features include essential elements such as acceptance criteria, dependencies, and design artifacts, which collectively ensure clarity and successful implementation. Additionally, a Feature can be described as a high-level capability or functionality, often articulated through Behavior-Driven Development (BDD) scenarios, which provide a structured approach to defining and validating its behavior. This dual perspective highlights both its strategic importance and its role in delivering tangible functionality.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cbea2cfe8c2736273a2f8aa2beb621f</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851943</data>
</node>
<node id="Epic">
  <data key="d0">Epic</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An Epic is a large, cross-cutting business or technical initiative in Agile development that spans multiple Agile Release Trains (ARTs) and Program Increments (PIs). It requires a clearly defined hypothesis or hypothesis statement, measurable business outcomes, and alignment with strategic themes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851945</data>
</node>
<node id="SAFe">
  <data key="d0">SAFe</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">SAFe (Scaled Agile Framework) is an organizational framework for scaling Agile practices across large enterprises, emphasizing built-in quality, alignment, and collaboration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851945</data>
</node>
<node id="Agile Release Train (ART)">
  <data key="d0">Agile Release Train (ART)</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">An Agile Release Train (ART) is a team of Agile teams that collaborate to deliver value in a synchronized and structured manner within a Program Increment. Operating within Agile frameworks, the ART focuses on planning, committing, and executing work collectively to achieve its objectives. This alignment ensures that all teams within the ART work cohesively to maximize value delivery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851948</data>
</node>
<node id="Program Increment (PI)">
  <data key="d0">Program Increment (PI)</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">A Program Increment (PI) is a time-boxed planning and execution cycle in SAFe, typically lasting 2–3 months, during which Agile teams deliver incremental value.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851948</data>
</node>
<node id="Backlog Refinement">
  <data key="d0">Backlog Refinement</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Backlog Refinement is an Agile event and meeting where teams collaboratively review, prioritize, and clarify work items, such as user stories, to ensure they are ready for development and implementation. This process helps teams adjust and refine the backlog, ensuring that work items are well-defined, properly prioritized, and prepared for upcoming sprints or development cycles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851963</data>
</node>
<node id="System Architect">
  <data key="d0">System Architect</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The System Architect is a technical leader responsible for defining, aligning, and communicating the overall architecture and design of systems within an Agile framework. This role involves ensuring that the technical architecture and enablers are effectively coordinated across teams to support the successful delivery of solutions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851965</data>
</node>
<node id="Product Owner">
  <data key="d0">Product Owner</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Product Owner is a key role in Agile development, responsible for defining and prioritizing the team backlog to ensure alignment with business goals and delivering maximum business value. This role involves prioritizing work, refining user stories, and maintaining a clear focus on business objectives to guide the development team effectively. The Product Owner plays a critical part in bridging the gap between stakeholders and the development team, ensuring that the team's efforts are aligned with organizational priorities and customer needs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851968</data>
</node>
<node id="Quality Assurance (QA)">
  <data key="d0">Quality Assurance (QA)</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Quality Assurance (QA) is a team or function responsible for validating that work items meet defined acceptance criteria and ensuring overall product quality.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851968</data>
</node>
<node id="Definition Of Ready (DoR)">
  <data key="d0">Definition Of Ready (DoR)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Definition Of Ready (DoR) is a set of criteria that ensures a work item is ready to be pulled into development. It applies to User Stories, Features, and Epics, ensuring clarity, testability, and alignment with business goals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851968</data>
</node>
<node id="INVEST Criteria">
  <data key="d0">INVEST Criteria</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The INVEST Criteria is a guideline and framework designed for creating well-formed User Stories in agile development. It emphasizes that User Stories should adhere to six key principles: they should be Independent, meaning they can stand alone without relying on other stories; Negotiable, allowing for discussion and refinement; Valuable, delivering clear benefits to stakeholders; Estimable, meaning their effort can be reasonably estimated; Small, ensuring they are manageable and can be completed within a short timeframe; and Testable, meaning their success can be objectively verified. The INVEST Criteria serves as a practical tool to ensure clarity, quality, and effectiveness in the creation of User Stories.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851971</data>
</node>
<node id="Acceptance Criteria">
  <data key="d0">Acceptance Criteria</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Acceptance Criteria are clear conditions that must be met for a story to be considered complete and testable.&lt;SEP&gt;Acceptance Criteria are specific conditions that a user story must meet to be considered complete, enabling testing and validation.&lt;SEP&gt;Acceptance Criteria are specific, clear, and testable conditions that a user story or work item must meet to be considered complete. They serve as a basis for testing and validation, ensuring that the story or work item is both complete and functional. Acceptance Criteria are often written in Behavior-Driven Development (BDD) format, using the structure of Given/When/Then, to provide clarity and consistency.&lt;SEP&gt;Acceptance Criteria are specific, testable conditions that a work item must meet to be considered complete. They are often written in BDD format (Given/When/Then).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cbea2cfe8c2736273a2f8aa2beb621f</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851971</data>
</node>
<node id="Business Value">
  <data key="d0">Business Value</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Business Value refers to the measurable benefits a work item delivers to stakeholders or customers, ensuring alignment with strategic goals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851971</data>
</node>
<node id="Enabler Stories">
  <data key="d0">Enabler Stories</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Enabler Stories are work items that support technical, architectural, or compliance needs, enabling the delivery of business value.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851971</data>
</node>
<node id="Program Backlog">
  <data key="d0">Program Backlog</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Program Backlog is a prioritized list of Features and Enabler Stories that are ready for implementation by Agile Release Trains.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851971</data>
</node>
<node id="Dependencies">
  <data key="d0">Dependencies</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Dependencies are relationships between work items, teams, or entities that require coordination to ensure successful delivery. They often involve cross-ART (Agile Release Train) or cross-vendor requirements that must be addressed to achieve the successful implementation of larger initiatives, such as Epics. Effective management of dependencies is critical for aligning efforts and ensuring seamless collaboration across different groups or organizations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851973</data>
</node>
<node id="Iteration">
  <data key="d0">Iteration</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">An Iteration is a time-boxed development cycle within Agile, typically lasting 1–2 weeks, during which teams deliver incremental value.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851973</data>
</node>
<node id="Built-In Quality">
  <data key="d0">Built-In Quality</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Built-In Quality is a principle in SAFe that emphasizes integrating quality practices into every step of the development process.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851973</data>
</node>
<node id="UX Inputs">
  <data key="d0">UX Inputs</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">UX Inputs refer to user experience artifacts such as mockups, wireframes, and design guidelines that inform the development of work items.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851973</data>
</node>
<node id="Planning Poker">
  <data key="d0">Planning Poker</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Planning Poker is a consensus-based estimation technique used by Agile teams to assign effort to work items.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851973</data>
</node>
<node id="Feature Backlog Refinement">
  <data key="d0">Feature Backlog Refinement</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Feature Backlog Refinement is an Agile event where teams review and clarify Features to ensure readiness for implementation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851973</data>
</node>
<node id="Business Owners">
  <data key="d0">Business Owners</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Business Owners are stakeholders who provide input on prioritization and ensure alignment of work items with strategic goals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851973</data>
</node>
<node id="Scaled Agile Framework (SAFe)">
  <data key="d0">Scaled Agile Framework (SAFe)</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Scaled Agile Framework (SAFe) is a comprehensive methodology designed to scale Agile practices across teams, organizations, and large enterprises. It emphasizes alignment, collaboration, and the delivery of value, making it particularly suited for complex environments. SAFe integrates Behavior-Driven Development (BDD) to support quality and enable continuous delivery, ensuring that organizations can achieve consistent and efficient outcomes while maintaining high standards of product and service quality.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cbea2cfe8c2736273a2f8aa2beb621f</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851978</data>
</node>
<node id="Agile Release Train Leadership">
  <data key="d0">Agile Release Train Leadership</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Agile Release Train Leadership includes roles such as Release Train Engineer (RTE), Product Manager, and System Architect, responsible for guiding ARTs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851978</data>
</node>
<node id="Non-Functional Requirements">
  <data key="d0">Non-Functional Requirements</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Non-Functional Requirements are constraints such as performance, security, and scalability that must be considered during development.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851978</data>
</node>
<node id="Epic Hypothesis Statement">
  <data key="d0">Epic Hypothesis Statement</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An Epic Hypothesis Statement is a structured format used to define the expected capability, measurable benefit, and success criteria for an Epic. It serves as a clear and concise framework to articulate the goals and anticipated outcomes of an Epic, ensuring alignment and focus on delivering value.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851981</data>
</node>
<node id="Strategic Themes">
  <data key="d0">Strategic Themes</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Strategic Themes are high-level business objectives that serve as a framework for guiding prioritization and alignment of work items. They play a critical role in steering portfolio-level initiatives and ensuring alignment with overarching organizational goals. By providing a clear focus, Strategic Themes help organizations maintain consistency and coherence in their strategic direction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851984</data>
</node>
<node id="OKRs">
  <data key="d0">OKRs</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">OKRs (Objectives and Key Results) are a framework for setting measurable goals and tracking progress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851984</data>
</node>
<node id="Wireframes">
  <data key="d0">Wireframes</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Wireframes are visual design artifacts that outline the structure and layout of a user interface.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-263dd2bc37bfae14dc8f9beff9e5d09e</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851984</data>
</node>
<node id="Portfolio Kanban">
  <data key="d0">Portfolio Kanban</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Portfolio Kanban is a system used to manage the flow of Epics through different stages, such as Funnel, Analyzing, and Backlog.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851984</data>
</node>
<node id="Lean Portfolio Management (LPM)">
  <data key="d0">Lean Portfolio Management (LPM)</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Lean Portfolio Management (LPM) is responsible for overseeing portfolio-level initiatives, ensuring alignment with strategic goals, and managing budgets and guardrails.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851984</data>
</node>
<node id="Minimum Viable Product (MVP)">
  <data key="d0">Minimum Viable Product (MVP)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Minimum Viable Product (MVP) is the smallest set of features that delivers value and validates the hypothesis of an Epic.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851984</data>
</node>
<node id="Release Train Engineer (RTE)">
  <data key="d0">Release Train Engineer (RTE)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Release Train Engineer (RTE) is a servant leader and coach for the Agile Release Train, facilitating processes and alignment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851984</data>
</node>
<node id="OKRs (Objectives and Key Results)">
  <data key="d0">OKRs (Objectives and Key Results)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">OKRs are a goal-setting framework used to define and measure outcomes, ensuring alignment with strategic priorities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851984</data>
</node>
<node id="Epic Owner">
  <data key="d0">Epic Owner</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Epic Owner is responsible for shepherding an Epic through the Portfolio Kanban system, ensuring alignment and stakeholder engagement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851984</data>
</node>
<node id="Business Outcome">
  <data key="d0">Business Outcome</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Business Outcome refers to the measurable and articulated results expected from implementing an Epic, aligned with strategic themes and OKRs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851984</data>
</node>
<node id="Strategic Fit">
  <data key="d0">Strategic Fit</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Strategic Fit ensures that an Epic aligns with strategic themes, OKRs, and the portfolio vision to deliver value.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851984</data>
</node>
<node id="Lean Business Case">
  <data key="d0">Lean Business Case</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Lean Business Case is a document that outlines the problem statement, business impact, success criteria, cost of delay, and forecasted MVP scope and cost for an Epic.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851984</data>
</node>
<node id="Enabler Epics">
  <data key="d0">Enabler Epics</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Enabler Epics are supporting initiatives or architectural work required to deliver the main Epic, often involving cross-team or cross-vendor dependencies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851984</data>
</node>
<node id="Investment Guardrails">
  <data key="d0">Investment Guardrails</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Investment Guardrails are portfolio-level constraints, such as budget caps and capacity allocations, that ensure Epics fit within organizational boundaries.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851984</data>
</node>
<node id="Value Stream">
  <data key="d0">Value Stream</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Value Stream refers to the sequence of activities required to deliver value, often used for coordinating high-level capabilities and features.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851984</data>
</node>
<node id="WSJF Estimation">
  <data key="d0">WSJF Estimation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">WSJF (Weighted Shortest Job First) Estimation is a prioritization method used to rank Epics and features based on cost of delay and job size.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851984</data>
</node>
<node id="Portfolio Sync">
  <data key="d0">Portfolio Sync</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Portfolio Sync is a meeting where stakeholders review and align on Epics, ensuring readiness to transition through the Portfolio Kanban stages.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851984</data>
</node>
<node id="Epic Review Board">
  <data key="d0">Epic Review Board</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Epic Review Board is a group responsible for reviewing Epics and approving their transition into the Portfolio Backlog.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851984</data>
</node>
<node id="Story">
  <data key="d0">Story</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Story is a small, self-contained unit of work that delivers value and adheres to the INVEST criteria.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851984</data>
</node>
<node id="PI Planning">
  <data key="d0">PI Planning</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">PI (Program Increment) Planning is a collaborative event where teams align on objectives, features, and dependencies for the upcoming increment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851984</data>
</node>
<node id="Vertical Slices">
  <data key="d0">Vertical Slices</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Vertical Slices are smaller, manageable portions of a user story that are broken down into end-to-end deliverables. These slices encompass all layers of functionality required for the feature, including the user interface (UI), backend, and integration components. By dividing large stories into these comprehensive, self-contained units, Vertical Slices ensure that each segment is fully functional and contributes to the overall system, facilitating efficient development and delivery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea164965a4c87b027e0d1fd2a759195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cbea2cfe8c2736273a2f8aa2beb621f</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851987</data>
</node>
<node id="Stakeholders">
  <data key="d0">Stakeholders</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Stakeholders are individuals or groups involved in refining stories during backlog grooming or planning, ensuring alignment with business goals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4cbea2cfe8c2736273a2f8aa2beb621f</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851987</data>
</node>
<node id="Developers">
  <data key="d0">Developers</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Developers are responsible for implementing code and collaborating with stakeholders and testers to refine and deliver user stories.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4cbea2cfe8c2736273a2f8aa2beb621f</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851987</data>
</node>
<node id="Testers">
  <data key="d0">Testers</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Testers validate the functionality of user stories, ensuring they meet acceptance criteria and align with the definition of done.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4cbea2cfe8c2736273a2f8aa2beb621f</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851987</data>
</node>
<node id="Product Owners">
  <data key="d0">Product Owners</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Product Owners prioritize work based on ROI and ensure that user stories deliver business value.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4cbea2cfe8c2736273a2f8aa2beb621f</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851987</data>
</node>
<node id="Behavior-Driven Development (BDD)">
  <data key="d0">Behavior-Driven Development (BDD)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">BDD is an Agile software development practice that emphasizes collaboration among stakeholders, developers, and testers to define system behavior using concrete examples.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4cbea2cfe8c2736273a2f8aa2beb621f</data>
  <data key="d4">./data/US/User_Stories\agile terms and definitions.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1752851987</data>
</node>
<node id="Test-Driven Development (TDD)">
  <data key="d0">Test-Driven Development (TDD)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">TDD is a software development practice focused on writing tests before code, which BDD extends by emphasizing behavior over implementation.</data>
